      "writtenOn": "2023-04-24T16:50:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks for checking!\n\n\u003e So AFAICT we need swap_bytes() here rather than to_be() or to_le()\n\nI agree that the SMCCC states that the respective order of the registers is that W0 holds the lowest-order word and that registers hold their respective word in native order (\"_Bytes 0…3 with byte 0 in the low-order bits_\").\n\nFrom there, I derive that the value\n```\nlet uuid_ne \u003d (res[3] as u32 as u128 \u003c\u003c 96)\n    | (res[2] as u32 as u128 \u003c\u003c 64)\n    | (res[1] as u32 as u128 \u003c\u003c 32)\n    | (res[0] as u32 as u128);\n```\nthen holds the desired UUID in **native order**, for any endianness. In turn, this implies that if we want to pass that value to a function that expects BE (such as `Uuid::from_u128`), it first needs to be byte-swapped on LE but should be kept as-is on BE i.e.\n```\nUUid::from_u128(uuid_ne.to_be())\n```\nNote that, on LE, this is equivalent to your implementation (`u128::to_be` \u003d\u003d `u32::byte_swap` then `(r[0] \u003c\u003c 96) | ... | r[3]`) but not on BE.",
      "parentUuid": "6b3a9fc0_7395c11d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 85,
        "startChar": 9,
        "endLine": 88,
        "endChar": 31
      },
      "revId": "d4b9a08a208f20bbd3927771ccdf99111cc0193e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
